  "id": "number-of-1-bits",
  "questionId": "number-of-1-bits",
  "slug": "number-of-1-bits",
  "title": "Number of 1 Bits",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Bit manipulation",
  "patterns": [
    "bit manipulation",
    "hamming weight"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "blind-75",
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "instead of checking all 32 bit positions even when most are 0, can you skip straight to where the set bits are?",
    "what does n & (n - 1) do to the binary representation of n?",
    "n - 1 flips all the bits after (and including) the lowest set bit of n - so n & (n - 1) clears exactly that lowest set bit",
    "repeatedly apply n = n & (n - 1) and count how many times you can do it before n becomes 0 - that count is the number of set bits"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "always looping a fixed 32 times instead of the faster Brian Kernighan approach that loops once per set bit",
    "off-by-one errors when shifting and masking with & 1",
    "not handling n = 0 (the loop should simply never execute and return 0)"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a non-negative integer `n`.\n\n**Return the number of set bits (1s) in its binary representation.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= n <= 2^31 - 1",
      "n is represented using 32 bits",
      "the answer (count of set bits) is between 0 and 32"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "n = 0 (no set bits)",
      "n is a power of two (exactly one set bit)",
      "n has every bit set (e.g. 2^31 - 1)"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
